# 44. Real-World Example — Company Project

Imagine a company has:

```text
project/
├── source/
├── reports/
├── secrets/
└── backup/
```

Users:

```text
developer
manager
intern
```

Requirements:

```text
Developer → Read + Write source
Manager   → Read + Write everything
Intern    → Read source
Intern    → No access to secrets
```

Linux permissions can be used to enforce these rules.

For example:

```text
source/
Owner  → rwx
Group  → rwx
Others → r-x
```

And:

```text
secrets/
Owner  → rwx
Group  → ---
Others → ---
```

This is how Linux permissions can be used in real organizations.

---

# 45. Real-World Example — Web Server

Imagine a Linux web server:

```text
/var/www/html
```

It contains:

```text
index.html
config.php
uploads/
```

We might want:

```text
index.html → readable by web server
config.php → protected
uploads/    → writable only when required
```

Incorrect permissions could allow unauthorized modification.

For example, making the entire web directory writable by everyone:

```bash
chmod -R 777 /var/www/html
```

would be dangerous in many environments.

A better approach is to assign only the permissions required by the web application.

---

# 46. Real-World Example — SSH Private Key

SSH private keys are sensitive.

A private key should generally not be readable by other users.

For example:

```bash
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
```

This means:

```text
Owner → Read + Write
Group → No access
Others → No access
```

This protects the private key from other local users.

---

# 47. Real-World Example — Shared Team Directory

Suppose a company has:

```text
/team/project
```

The development team needs to work together.

We can create a group:

```bash
sudo groupadd developers
```

Add users:

```bash
sudo usermod -aG developers alice
sudo usermod -aG developers bob
```

Change the directory group:

```bash
sudo chgrp developers /team/project
```

Give the group appropriate permissions:

```bash
sudo chmod 2770 /team/project
```

The `2` sets SGID.

This helps newly created files inherit the directory's group.

