# 67. Important Security Mistakes to Avoid

### Mistake 1 — Using 777 Everywhere

```bash
chmod -R 777 directory
```

This can unnecessarily expose files to modification.

---

### Mistake 2 — Giving Too Many sudo Privileges

Users should only receive administrative privileges they actually need.

---

### Mistake 3 — Making Sensitive Files World-Readable

Avoid:

```text
-rw-r--r--
```

for files containing secrets when other users should not have access.

Use an appropriately restrictive permission such as:

```text
-rw-------
```

when only the owner needs access.

---

### Mistake 4 — Ignoring SUID Files

Unexpected SUID programs should be investigated.

---

### Mistake 5 — Blind Recursive Changes

Be careful with:

```bash
chmod -R
chown -R
```

Always verify the target before executing the command.

